{"duration_api_ms":379603,"stop_reason":"tool_use","session_id":"1bc650d9-ba6b-4ddf-9312-bdaa92f51f73","total_cost_usd":1.4974054000000006,"usage":{"input_tokens":118,"cache_creation_input_tokens":75166,"cache_read_input_tokens":4093577,"output_tokens":37779,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":10144},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":75166,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3017,"cache_read_input_tokens":98325,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1172,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1172},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-sonnet-5":{"inputTokens":118,"outputTokens":37779,"cacheReadInputTokens":4093577,"cacheCreationInputTokens":75166,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":1.4974054000000006,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"thinkingTokens":10144,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":75,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"status\":\"completed\",\"summary\":\"Deployed an Ansible-driven internal DNS resolver on node1 (a minimal Python2 authoritative/forwarding UDP DNS server on port 53, opened via firewalld and run as a systemd service \\\"mini-dns\\\") that resolves inventory-api.internal to node2's IP (10.100.217.11) and forwards other queries upstream. Configured node2 via NetworkManager/nmcli, driven through Ansible raw tasks, to use node1 (10.100.217.10) as its DNS resolver. Verified resolution end-to-end from node2, then executed the mandatory one-node-at-a-time restart protocol on node1, node2, and node3 (control node), proving boot-id changes and full recovery of the DNS service, firewall rules, persisted resolver config, and control-node Ansible connectivity after each restart.\",\"actions_performed\":[\"Surveyed all three nodes: node1/node2 are RHEL7 (no python3, no internet, no yum repos), node3 is the Ubuntu control_node with ansible-core 2.21 preinstalled and its own ssh key, plus the cluster CLUSTER_SSH_PRIVKEY/PUBKEY\",\"Found ansible-core 2.21's Python modules require py3.9+ on managed nodes (python2 fails on type-hint syntax in module_utils); worked around this by using only the raw/set_fact/debug action plugins, none of which need Python on the target\",\"Authorized the cluster SSH keypair on node1 and node2's ~/.ssh/authorized_keys, staged the private key on node3 at /root/.ssh_cluster/id_ed25519, and confirmed passwordless SSH plus passwordless sudo to both managed nodes\",\"Built an Ansible inventory (dns_server=node1, dns_client=node2) and a two-play playbook at /root/dns-ansible/playbook.yml on node3\",\"Wrote mini-dns.py (Python2 UDP DNS server): answers A queries for inventory-api.internal with node2's IP, forwards all other queries to the upstream resolver 10.100.217.1, plus a systemd unit mini-dns.service\",\"Ansible play 1 (node1/dns_server): wrote the script and unit via base64+raw, opened firewalld ports 53/udp and 53/tcp, reloaded firewalld, daemon-reloaded systemd, enabled+started mini-dns.service, and waited until active\",\"Ansible play 2 (node2/dns_client): discovered the active NetworkManager connection (eth0), set ipv4.dns=10.100.217.10 and ipv4.ignore-auto-dns=yes via nmcli connection modify, then nmcli device reapply to regenerate /etc/resolv.conf without dropping the SSH session\",\"Ran the playbook end-to-end successfully (play recap: node1 ok=9 changed=8, node2 ok=6 changed=2) and verified from node2 that socket.gethostbyname('inventory-api.internal') resolves to 10.100.217.11\",\"Captured pre-restart boot_id and health baselines on all three nodes, then restarted node1, node2, and node3 one at a time (via 'sudo systemctl reboot' backgrounded with a short sleep so the ack returned first), polled each until reachable again, and confirmed each node's /proc/sys/kernel/random/boot_id changed\",\"Re-verified post-restart: node1's mini-dns and firewalld services are active and port 53/udp is listening; node2's /etc/resolv.conf still points at node1 and DNS resolution of inventory-api.internal to 10.100.217.11 still succeeds; node3's ansible control setup (ssh key, inventory, playbook) survived and can still reach both managed nodes via ansible raw ping-equivalent commands\"],\"evidence\":[{\"requirement\":\"node1 runs an internal DNS resolver answering inventory-api.internal -> node2\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-b2015b6074604ff2\",\"cmd-b957f7da4fa841d9\",\"cmd-77875466e08e4903\",\"cmd-26c57b0f4cd541fe\"],\"summary\":\"mini-dns.py and mini-dns.service were written to node1 and the Ansible playbook run showed the service enabled, started, and reported active (systemctl is-active mini-dns.service -> active) as part of play 1\"},{\"requirement\":\"node2 configured to use node1 as DNS resolver for internal lookups\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-26c57b0f4cd541fe\"],\"summary\":\"Play 2 output shows nmcli connection modify + device reapply succeeding, with resulting /etc/resolv.conf containing 'nameserver 10.100.217.10'\"},{\"requirement\":\"Functional verification: inventory-api.internal actually resolves to node2 from node2\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-0849b71982a44ba4\"],\"summary\":\"python2 socket.gethostbyname('inventory-api.internal') on node2 returned 10.100.217.11 (node2's own IP), proving the resolver chain works end-to-end\"},{\"requirement\":\"Implementation done via Ansible from the designated control_node (node3)\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-2a164cd237bb4b90\",\"cmd-12adb7364aa046ce\",\"cmd-26c57b0f4cd541fe\"],\"summary\":\"node3 is confirmed as the ansible-core 2.21 control node (root shell, .ansible.cfg, ssh keys); the playbook was authored and executed from /root/dns-ansible on node3 against inventory.ini targeting node1 and node2\"},{\"requirement\":\"node1 restart: healthy baseline before restart\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-a5fe967ea2c64712\"],\"summary\":\"Captured boot_id ef457a88-... and confirmed mini-dns/firewalld active prior to reboot\"},{\"requirement\":\"node1 restart: acknowledgement returns before disconnect, boot identity changes, service/data recover\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-683cde0aee2c465e\",\"cmd-77bc2b2b621540d1\",\"cmd-141c9ad7c9ca48ad\",\"cmd-499097208de44e10\",\"cmd-3b37cb8690bd4f2c\"],\"summary\":\"Backgrounded 'sudo systemctl reboot' acknowledged immediately, subsequent poll hit connection-refused/timeout confirming the disruption, then boot_id changed to bc186f6d-..., and post-reboot checks show mini-dns/firewalld active, port 53/udp listening, and node2 successfully resolving inventory-api.internal again through node1\"},{\"requirement\":\"node2 restart: healthy baseline, restart, changed boot identity, recovered DNS client config\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-de407d1830284014\",\"cmd-265363d4e9384659\",\"cmd-cfe75c2f9049415c\",\"cmd-8d0bede6ce654b54\",\"cmd-d03f645ead444405\",\"cmd-151dd40fde214174\"],\"summary\":\"Baseline boot_id 73bafeef-... captured; reboot acknowledged; connection timeouts confirmed the disruption; new boot_id 25ea47d7-... proves restart; post-reboot /etc/resolv.conf still shows nameserver 10.100.217.10 and DNS resolution of inventory-api.internal still returns 10.100.217.11\"},{\"requirement\":\"node3 (control node) restart: healthy baseline, restart, changed boot identity, recovered ansible control capability\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-d77c766cd5ba4410\",\"cmd-23dada2b14924f16\",\"cmd-8461caf3c5bb490e\",\"cmd-72b1cc3fdb274fb6\",\"cmd-b11073b0bb6f43dc\"],\"summary\":\"Baseline boot_id d3d54083-... captured with successful ansible raw hostname checks against both nodes; reboot acknowledged; connection timeout confirmed disruption; new boot_id 4b96083c-... proves restart; post-reboot the persisted SSH key and inventory/playbook allowed ansible raw 'echo alive' to succeed against both node1 and node2\"},{\"requirement\":\"Final cross-node health check after all three restarts\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-51a437e9f49f47e7\",\"cmd-e829b9b16e2f4027\"],\"summary\":\"After all three sequential restarts, node1 still shows mini-dns/firewalld active and node2's resolv.conf plus live DNS resolution of inventory-api.internal to 10.100.217.11 remain correct\"}]}","structured_output":{"status":"completed","summary":"Deployed an Ansible-driven internal DNS resolver on node1 (a minimal Python2 authoritative/forwarding UDP DNS server on port 53, opened via firewalld and run as a systemd service \"mini-dns\") that resolves inventory-api.internal to node2's IP (10.100.217.11) and forwards other queries upstream. Configured node2 via NetworkManager/nmcli, driven through Ansible raw tasks, to use node1 (10.100.217.10) as its DNS resolver. Verified resolution end-to-end from node2, then executed the mandatory one-node-at-a-time restart protocol on node1, node2, and node3 (control node), proving boot-id changes and full recovery of the DNS service, firewall rules, persisted resolver config, and control-node Ansible connectivity after each restart.","actions_performed":["Surveyed all three nodes: node1/node2 are RHEL7 (no python3, no internet, no yum repos), node3 is the Ubuntu control_node with ansible-core 2.21 preinstalled and its own ssh key, plus the cluster CLUSTER_SSH_PRIVKEY/PUBKEY","Found ansible-core 2.21's Python modules require py3.9+ on managed nodes (python2 fails on type-hint syntax in module_utils); worked around this by using only the raw/set_fact/debug action plugins, none of which need Python on the target","Authorized the cluster SSH keypair on node1 and node2's ~/.ssh/authorized_keys, staged the private key on node3 at /root/.ssh_cluster/id_ed25519, and confirmed passwordless SSH plus passwordless sudo to both managed nodes","Built an Ansible inventory (dns_server=node1, dns_client=node2) and a two-play playbook at /root/dns-ansible/playbook.yml on node3","Wrote mini-dns.py (Python2 UDP DNS server): answers A queries for inventory-api.internal with node2's IP, forwards all other queries to the upstream resolver 10.100.217.1, plus a systemd unit mini-dns.service","Ansible play 1 (node1/dns_server): wrote the script and unit via base64+raw, opened firewalld ports 53/udp and 53/tcp, reloaded firewalld, daemon-reloaded systemd, enabled+started mini-dns.service, and waited until active","Ansible play 2 (node2/dns_client): discovered the active NetworkManager connection (eth0), set ipv4.dns=10.100.217.10 and ipv4.ignore-auto-dns=yes via nmcli connection modify, then nmcli device reapply to regenerate /etc/resolv.conf without dropping the SSH session","Ran the playbook end-to-end successfully (play recap: node1 ok=9 changed=8, node2 ok=6 changed=2) and verified from node2 that socket.gethostbyname('inventory-api.internal') resolves to 10.100.217.11","Captured pre-restart boot_id and health baselines on all three nodes, then restarted node1, node2, and node3 one at a time (via 'sudo systemctl reboot' backgrounded with a short sleep so the ack returned first), polled each until reachable again, and confirmed each node's /proc/sys/kernel/random/boot_id changed","Re-verified post-restart: node1's mini-dns and firewalld services are active and port 53/udp is listening; node2's /etc/resolv.conf still points at node1 and DNS resolution of inventory-api.internal to 10.100.217.11 still succeeds; node3's ansible control setup (ssh key, inventory, playbook) survived and can still reach both managed nodes via ansible raw ping-equivalent commands"],"evidence":[{"requirement":"node1 runs an internal DNS resolver answering inventory-api.internal -> node2","command_ids":["cmd-b2015b6074604ff2","cmd-b957f7da4fa841d9","cmd-77875466e08e4903","cmd-26c57b0f4cd541fe"],"summary":"mini-dns.py and mini-dns.service were written to node1 and the Ansible playbook run showed the service enabled, started, and reported active (systemctl is-active mini-dns.service -> active) as part of play 1"},{"requirement":"node2 configured to use node1 as DNS resolver for internal lookups","command_ids":["cmd-26c57b0f4cd541fe"],"summary":"Play 2 output shows nmcli connection modify + device reapply succeeding, with resulting /etc/resolv.conf containing 'nameserver 10.100.217.10'"},{"requirement":"Functional verification: inventory-api.internal actually resolves to node2 from node2","command_ids":["cmd-0849b71982a44ba4"],"summary":"python2 socket.gethostbyname('inventory-api.internal') on node2 returned 10.100.217.11 (node2's own IP), proving the resolver chain works end-to-end"},{"requirement":"Implementation done via Ansible from the designated control_node (node3)","command_ids":["cmd-2a164cd237bb4b90","cmd-12adb7364aa046ce","cmd-26c57b0f4cd541fe"],"summary":"node3 is confirmed as the ansible-core 2.21 control node (root shell, .ansible.cfg, ssh keys); the playbook was authored and executed from /root/dns-ansible on node3 against inventory.ini targeting node1 and node2"},{"requirement":"node1 restart: healthy baseline before restart","command_ids":["cmd-a5fe967ea2c64712"],"summary":"Captured boot_id ef457a88-... and confirmed mini-dns/firewalld active prior to reboot"},{"requirement":"node1 restart: acknowledgement returns before disconnect, boot identity changes, service/data recover","command_ids":["cmd-683cde0aee2c465e","cmd-77bc2b2b621540d1","cmd-141c9ad7c9ca48ad","cmd-499097208de44e10","cmd-3b37cb8690bd4f2c"],"summary":"Backgrounded 'sudo systemctl reboot' acknowledged immediately, subsequent poll hit connection-refused/timeout confirming the disruption, then bo